<section class="shell band">
  <p class="eyebrow">Blog &middot; October 2026</p>
  <h1>What makes supervision trauma-informed</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>"Trauma-informed" has been attached to so many things that it has started to mean very
    little. In supervision it has a specific meaning, and it is not that the supervisor knows
    about trauma. It is that the supervision hour treats the supervisee's own nervous system as
    part of the clinical picture rather than as an interruption to it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>Three things it changes</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>What gets brought.</strong> In supervision that is not trauma-informed, the
    cases that get presented are the interesting ones and the ones where the associate already
    has a formulation. The cases that actually need the hour are the ones where the associate
    felt stupid, frozen, irritated with the client, or bored. Those get left out, because
    bringing them means admitting to a reaction. A trauma-informed supervisor asks for those
    first and makes it survivable to bring them.</li>

    <li><strong>What counts as data.</strong> If you came out of a session with your jaw tight
    and no memory of the last ten minutes, that is information about what happened in the room,
    not a sign you are bad at this. Countertransference is the oldest idea in the field and it
    is still routinely treated as a personal failing in supervision. Your activation is often
    the clearest read available on a client who cannot yet say what is happening to them.</li>

    <li><strong>How fast the hour moves.</strong> A supervisor who piles on technique when a
    supervisee is already overwhelmed is doing the thing we all tell clients not to do. Pacing
    applies here too. Sometimes the useful hour is one piece of understanding and nothing else,
    and the rest waits.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>Why it matters more for associates</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>An LPC Associate is carrying a caseload, learning the work, and being evaluated by
        the person they most need to be honest with. That is a structural bind. The supervisor
        signs the hours, which means the person who decides whether you qualify is the same
        person you are supposed to tell when you got it wrong.</p>
        <p>You cannot remove that bind. You can name it out loud, early, so that the associate
        is not quietly managing it alone for three thousand hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Developmental trauma shows up in supervision the same way it shows up anywhere else:
        as difficulty asking for what you need from someone with power over you. The
        <a href="/approach/narm/">NARM</a> frame is as useful on this side of the chair as it
        is with clients.</p>
