<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about trauma-informed supervision</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>What makes supervision trauma-informed rather than just supportive?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Supportive supervision is kind about your reactions. Trauma-informed supervision uses
      them. The difference shows up in what happens after you say a client made you angry: being
      reassured, or being asked what that anger is telling you about the client's history and
      about your own.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is trauma-informed supervision only useful if I work with trauma clients?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. The approach is about how the supervision hour is run, not about the diagnosis on
      the caseload. An associate seeing anxiety and adjustment presentations still has a nervous
      system that responds to sitting with distress forty hours a week.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Does trauma-informed supervision turn into therapy for the supervisee?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>It should not, and the line matters. Supervision looks at your reaction insofar as it
      bears on the clinical work and then stops. If what surfaces needs more than that, the
      answer is your own therapy with someone who is not signing your hours.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I bring a case I think I handled badly?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Those are the ones worth the hour. A supervisee who only brings cases that went well is
      being supervised on the wrong material, and the supervisor learns nothing about how they
      actually practise.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
